WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.022
Cloud asset inventory is the foundation
for attack surface documentation.

2
00:00:08.102 --> 00:00:15.976
The assessment should identify cloud
services, workloads, APIs, identities,

3
00:00:16.056 --> 00:00:21.110
storage locations, databases, public
endpoints,

4
00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:29.417
third-party integrations, internet-facing
resources, management interfaces,

5
00:00:29.497 --> 00:00:34.081
owners, environments, and data
sensitivity.

6
00:00:34.161 --> 00:00:40.978
Without this baseline, risk review becomes
scattered and reactive.

7
00:00:41.058 --> 00:00:44.701
Cloud names are often inconsistent.

8
00:00:44.781 --> 00:00:51.598
A resource tag may use one service name, a
repository may use another,

9
00:00:51.678 --> 00:00:56.496
and a diagram may use a business-friendly
label.

10
00:00:56.576 --> 00:01:02.688
AI can help normalize these names, group
related components,

11
00:01:02.768 --> 00:01:06.059
and identify likely duplicates.

12
00:01:06.139 --> 00:01:14.366
Human review is still required because
merging two similar-looking resources can

13
00:01:14.446 --> 00:01:21.028
hide important differences in ownership,
exposure, or data type.

14
00:01:21.108 --> 00:01:27.689
Attack surface documentation should
connect assets to exposure.

15
00:01:27.769 --> 00:01:34.233
A compute workload matters differently if
it has public ingress,

16
00:01:34.313 --> 00:01:43.128
privileged identity access, sensitive data
access, or management-plane permissions.

17
00:01:43.208 --> 00:01:50.847
A storage location matters differently if
it is private, shared internally,

18
00:01:50.927 --> 00:01:56.686
exposed publicly, or connected to a
third-party process.

19
00:01:56.766 --> 00:02:01.585
Context turns inventory into security
insight.

20
00:02:01.665 --> 00:02:08.247
AI can help build attack surface
registers, open-question lists,

21
00:02:08.327 --> 00:02:10.912
and owner review packets.

22
00:02:10.992 --> 00:02:17.927
A useful register might include component
name, environment, owner,

23
00:02:18.007 --> 00:02:24.941
public exposure, authentication method,
identities, data handled,

24
00:02:25.021 --> 00:02:31.603
dependencies, evidence source,
assumptions, and review status.

25
00:02:31.683 --> 00:02:38.382
This structure makes the document easier
to validate and maintain.

26
00:02:38.462 --> 00:02:46.101
The output should be practical for
developers, security teams, architects,

27
00:02:46.181 --> 00:02:47.827
and responders.

28
00:02:47.907 --> 00:02:51.785
A polished diagram alone is not enough.

29
00:02:51.865 --> 00:02:59.505
The team needs a record that supports
review, prioritization, remediation,

30
00:02:59.585 --> 00:03:02.640
and future incident response.

31
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.479
The best inventory is one that can be
questioned, updated,

32
00:03:08.559 --> 00:03:11.380
and traced back to evidence.
